ng docker to do this  the current quality control checks  at a very high level  and then uh the metadata file this is an  important piece of the puzzle you need  to specify a metadata file  and the uh called metadata.json and its  contents need to be structured like this  and this essentially tells  the validation pipeline where your files  are and what the file names are  additionally this talks about  how you check processing status  it shows the  validation pipeline  talks about all the checks in the  validation pipeline and at a high level  what the checks do  and it talks about the output files and  the results from the validation pipeline  and then we've included some sample  studies one without validation errors  and a few with validation errors  so we talk about and those are all based  on the thousand genomes project  so there's also a link  to what the thousand genomes project is  right there  so going back to the previous readme  file  and remember we've gone through our  prerequisites so we're good there  and then we go to our setup so the first  thing we do is clone the repository from  github  let's go ahead and do that  and then we  cd to the directory that just got  created when we  cloned the repository  the next step is we're going to make an  output directory  that we're going to use to output the  results from the validation pipeline  from our sample study  and then we're going to change the  permissions on that output directory to  make sure docker doesn't have a problem  writing to that output directory  that's it now we're all set up we're  ready to run the validation pipeline  so what we've included in this repo is a  bash script which essentially start uses  docker compose to start all the  necessary containers that run  the infrastructure for the validation  pipeline  there's some important switches here you  need to know  there's a dash i switch  this is the input directory where your  files are located  the o switch this is the output  directory  where you want the validation results to  go into  and the dash m switch  essentially is the metadata.json file  that i talked about earlier which says  where  are your specific files  what are their file names of the files  that are located in your input directory  so i'm going to go ahead and copy this  and paste it in here  and start that  script running  so again it's a docker compose script  what it does is it pulls down  the ncbi slash gap tools container which  has our validation pipeline in it  it also pulls down several other  containers to build the infrastructure  to run the validation pipeline  we use airflow  to run the validation pipeline so  essentially what this is doing is  building an airflow  infrastructure on your docker host  so it  pulls down  a database container  a key value store container  a flower container so you can view your  jobs  the airflow web container which is the  user interface into the airflow  infrastructure  a scheduler  airflow scheduler  the gap tools ui which is the ui into  the gap tools validation results  and then the airflow worker container  so once all those containers are pulled  down  then  begins to start the containers  it starts the database and all the  associated comp components around  airflow  it then  installs the validation pipeline into  airflow  as a as what uh airflow calls a dag a  distributed  graph  and then it initializes the database it  initializes airflow it brings all the  containers up and it makes sure that  everything is running correctly  so it uh checks every so often to make  sure  until the airflow web server is running  which is the final piece so it knows  once the airflow web server is running  then the infrastructure is good to go  and once the infrastructure is good to  go then it kicks off the validation  pipeline with the parameters you gave it  earlier  once this is done  it gives you where you can go to visit  the gap tools ui  and it also gives a command for what you  d