 or a subset  of these files  and  the tool outputs qc results  uh some metrics and logging and some  some reports  if you have questions about specifics on  this the  the files within dbgap you can go to our  submission guide and the link is here  more about dbgap  tools the current output uh  returns specific errors and warnings of  the validation um  and  so  some of the  categories  of errors and warnings are file format  data set to data dictionary matching  subject and sample id matching across  files  and variable naming  and the geno sex and relationships to  the phenotypic data  and then we have this that we have two  reports that are reported out through  the pipeline  when appropriate  the genetic relationship and the x  chromosome sex determination  as computed by the tool  so you know there is two validation  modes for dbgap tools one is we run this  tool internally on the files that you  submit to dbgap through our submission  portal it's it's as of this year it's  fully integrated with submission portal  so when you upload files  it will run on all  eligible dbgap submissions  um  what the when the tool is run an  automated uh email of the results is  then sent to the submitter  and this tool also within the pipeline  internal pipeline compares the  previously submitted files if they  haven't been deleted from the submission  uh this allows us to do uh continuity  across versions  um  but this tool also can be run in a  standalone mode um it's set up as a  docker container which is click and  cloud ready  um  it validates files prior to submission  and this is the purpose of this is to  reduce iterations with dbgap you don't  have to submit the files you can check  them yourself if if you're so inclined  it is a little bit technical  um but it does run the same initials qc  check says dbgap now we do have a manual  curation  pipeline that is  more expansive  than this tool  but we are building out the tool to  include what we can automatically  um now i'd like to  transition over and allow uh  software engineer dale conklin to  show a demo of gap tools  hello this is dale conklin with the  national center for biotechnology  information which is part of the  national library of medicine at nih  i'm on the software development team and  have developed  with my team a tool that essentially  takes the validation pipeline for dbgap  submissions and makes it externally  available for anybody to download  the place to get started with this is to  go to  github.com to the ncbi organization and  the gaap tools repo  so the url you would go to is right here  and the first thing you take a look at  is the readme file  the readme file talks about how to get  gap tools running  on your local environment  the first thing you'll need is docker  installed  the next thing you'll need is to have  docker compose installed  and you'll also need access to your data  files if they're  local on  your host that you're going to be  running docker on you're fine if they're  located on s3 or gcs on the google cloud  platform  then you'll need to  essentially make those files locally  available to your docker host so we've  included some links here  for you to be able to go ahead and do  that  and the final prerequisite is to make  sure nothing on your computer is running  on port  8080  so to do that you take this command  run it on your host  and just make sure  nothing returns  in this case these connections are in  time weight and they're not actually  listening  if this were listening then that would  be a problem but since these are in time  weight they'll  go away soon and won't be a problem for  us  the next thing i want to go over is  before i go on to the setup is actually  the other readme file we have two  markdown files in here the other one is  called  gaaptools.md  and then this is a little more extensive  readme file  goes over what gap tools is it talks  about what is  been updated in the most recent version  why we're using docker to do this  the current quality control checks  at a very high level  and then uh the metadata file this is an  important piece of the puzzle you need  to specify a metadata file  and the uh called metadata.json and its  contents need to be structured like this  and this essentially tells  the validation pipeline where your files  are and what the file names are  additionally this talks about  how you check processing status  it shows the  validation pipeline  talks about all the checks in the  validation pipeline and at a high level  what the checks do  and it talks about the output files and  the results from the validation pipeline  and then we've included some sample  studies one without validation errors  and a few with validation errors  so we talk about and those are all based  on the thousand genomes project  so there's also a link  to what the thousand genomes project is  right there  so going back to the previous readme  file  and remember we've gone through our  prerequisites so we're good there  and then we go to our setup so the first  thing we do is clone the repository from  github  let's go ahead and do that  and then we  cd to the directory that just got  created when we  cloned the repository  the next step is we're going to make an  output directory  that we're going to use to output the  results from the validation pipeline  from our sample study  and then we're going to change the  permissions on that output directory to  make sure docker doesn't have a problem  writing to that output directory  that's it now we're all set up we're  ready to run the validation pipeline  so what we've included in this repo is a  bash script which essentially start uses  docker compose to start all the  necessary containers that run  the infrastructure for the validation  pipeline  there's some important switches here you  need to know  there's a dash i switch  this is the input directory where your  files are located  the o switch this is the output  directory  where you want the validation results to  go into  and the dash m switch  essentially is the metadata.json file  that i talked about earlier which says  where  are your specific files  what are their file names of the files  that are located in your input directory  so i'm going to go ahead and copy this  and paste it in here  and start that  script running  so again it's a docker compose script  what it does is it pulls down  the ncbi slash gap tools container which  has our validation pipeline in it  it also pulls down several other  containers to build the infrastructure  to run the validation pipeline  we use airflow  to run the validation pipeline so  essentially what this is doing is  building an airflow  infrastructure on your docker host  so it  pulls down  a database container  a key value store container  a flower container so you can view your  jobs  the airflow web container which is the  user interface into the airflow  infrastructure  a scheduler  airflow scheduler  the gap tools ui which is the ui into  the gap tools validation results  and then the airflow worker container  so once all those containers are pulled  down  then  begins to start the containers  it starts the database and all the  associated comp components around  airflow  it then  installs the validation pipeline into  airflow  as a as what uh airflow calls a dag a  distributed  graph  and then it initializes the database it  initializes airflow it brings all the  containers up and it makes sure that  everything is running correctly  so it uh checks every so often to make  sure  until the airflow web server is running  which is the final piece so it knows  once the airflow web server is running  then the infrastructure is good to go  and once the infrastructure is good to  go then it kicks off the validation  pipeline with the parameters you gave it  earlier  once this is done  it gives you where you can go to visit  the gap tools ui  and it also gives a command for what you  do when you're done with the containers  how to bring all those containers down  so i'm going to go ahead and go  into  the gap tools ui  there's an airflow web console link here  which takes you to the airflow web  console  and this is the validation pipeline  all these boxes are different tasks that  are run  to  validate the files that are part of your  submission  you can look at the status of each of  the tasks by looking at the color of the  line that's surrounding each task  if you hover over here under the success  you can see which tasks have been  completed successfully  the ones that are  highlighted in pink are ones that have  been skipped this particular study does  not have a pedigree file so all the  pedigree checks are skipped  and then the light green ones are the is  the task that's currently running  and then the ones that don't have a  status uh are  are uh there's no border around it or a  white border around it  so i'm gonna turn auto refresh back on  we can see that the validation pipeline  has completed so this once the  validation pipeline completes then now  we know that our validation results are  ready  so we go back to our main url  and under here  we see  the validation results  are right here there's a link  that you can click and it clicks to the  validation results we can see in this  particular study  the files processed have passed  and it gives you a link if you have any  uh need for any help you can click that  and email the db gap  sp help desk with any questions you have  you can see here there were zero files  with errors and three files with  warnings  the warnings this is a small study so  essentially the warnings are saying  there were insufficient x markers to  determine the subject's sex  and then there's also a validation  file that is produced called combined  one graph relationship  and i'll show you how to get to that in  just a minute  there's also a helpful link here that  you can go to  which  shows you all the validation errors that  could possibly occur and then a  description and the rule associated with  that error  so going back to  this email  these are the files that were analyzed  in the report and these are the files  that you've specified in your  metadata.json file  and then if you want to go back to the  home page you can go back to the home  page  so again in this  email there is this one file that's  produced that you can take a look at  you have to go into the  command line of your docker host  go to  the output directory  that you specified in your output  command line switch  and then in here you go to the geno  directory and then you go to the  graph relationship directory  and then you can see the file that was  produced as a result of this pipeline  and you can take a look at that file if  you like  so that is the  gap tools tool as it is today again a  good place to get started with this is  at github  slash ncbi slash gap tools  and oh one other thing i wanted to show  you was how to bring the  environment down go back to your  gaap tools  root directory of your github repo  and run  the dbgap docker dot bash command with  the with the down  argument afterwards  and that brings the environment down so  you don't have containers running on  your docker host that you don't need  anymore  so again so go to github.com  ncbi gap tools to get started with this  publicly available tool i appreciate you  taking the time to watch this  demonstration  and have a good day  thanks dale that was great um  uh  i think uh now we're at the the point in  this in the webinar where we can take  any and answer any questions yeah mike  do you want to show that slide uh the  the one at the end with the just uh help  desk stuff  so yeah mike we have some questions um  one of them was can you explain what the  subject consent file is  uh yes the subject consent file is in  its very simple form it's a three column  table um the first column is the subject  id of each uh  study pa